WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.199
LLM API endpoints should require authentication because the service consumes resources and may process sensitive data.

00:00:06.199 --> 00:00:08.487
API keys, service tokens, workload identity,

00:00:08.487 --> 00:00:12.680
or user-bound credentials can identify which caller is making a request.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.586
Authentication alone is not enough.

00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:19.542
A valid caller may still need limits on models, tenants, data sources, tools,

00:00:19.542 --> 00:00:21.829
request size, rate, or allowed operations.

00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:24.879
Authorization should be part of the application design,

00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:27.547
not an afterthought in the model prompt.

00:00:27.547 --> 00:00:32.122
A user should only be able to submit prompts against data and

00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:34.409
functions they are allowed to access.

00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:38.603
Tenant separation matters when the same service supports multiple customers, teams,

00:00:38.603 --> 00:00:39.365
or workspaces.

00:00:39.365 --> 00:00:43.558
Least privilege also applies to applications that call the LLM service.

00:00:43.558 --> 00:00:49.277
A small internal tool should not automatically receive broad access to every model and dataset.

00:00:49.277 --> 00:00:52.708
Exposure control is especially important for private inference endpoints.

00:00:52.708 --> 00:00:56.139
Local-only binding, LAN access, reverse proxies, TLS, network segmentation,

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:59.951
and firewall rules all influence who can reach the service.

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:04.525
A model endpoint that seems harmless on localhost can become a risk

00:01:04.525 --> 00:01:09.100
if it is accidentally bound to all interfaces or exposed through a

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:09.862
misconfigured proxy.

00:01:09.862 --> 00:01:13.293
Avoid unnecessary internet exposure for experimental or private services.

00:01:13.293 --> 00:01:17.487
Operational teams should know where credentials live, how they are rotated,

00:01:17.487 --> 00:01:20.917
how callers are revoked, and how usage is audited.

00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:25.111
Default keys, shared tokens, and undocumented service accounts create accountability problems.

00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:29.304
Strong API boundaries help prevent unauthorized resource use, accidental data exposure,

00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:34.260
and uncontrolled access to models that may be expensive or sensitive to operate.
